1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,018
This is ECE 320, video 9. The topic is data hazards and forwarding.

2
00:00:05,468 --> 00:00:17,931
After this video, you can find RAW, WAW and WAR pairs. You can decide how each value reaches its consumer. You can fill the forwarding and stall tables, and calculate the CPI with load-use stalls.

3
00:00:18,381 --> 00:00:32,420
Slide 15 defines a dependence between two instructions. In a data dependence, both use the same storage location. In a control dependence, one decides if the other executes. The older instruction must act first.

4
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:44,253
In a pipeline, it does not. Here, the add writes x3 in W, in cycle 5. But the subtract reads x3 in D, in cycle 3. That is before the write.

5
00:00:44,703 --> 00:00:53,177
A hazard is a dependence plus the chance of a wrong order. The pipeline must make sure that the effect of a wrong order is never visible outside.

6
00:00:53,627 --> 00:01:08,654
Slide 22 names three kinds of data dependence. In a read after write, or RAW, the younger instruction reads what the older one writes. It reads r3 in cycle 3, before the write in cycle 5. So RAW is a hazard.

7
00:01:09,104 --> 00:01:20,494
In a write after read, or WAR, the younger instruction writes a register that the older one reads. The read is in cycle 2, the write in cycle 6. The order is kept.

8
00:01:20,944 --> 00:01:30,169
In a write after write, or WAW, both instructions write the same register. Both write in W, in program order. The order is kept.

9
00:01:30,619 --> 00:01:40,041
So in our in-order 5-stage pipeline, only RAW causes a problem. Later, a long multiply makes WAW a problem too.

10
00:01:40,491 --> 00:01:53,865
Now a midterm question: fall 2019, question 1 part B. Find all RAW, WAW and WAR pairs in these four instructions. Pause the video and try it.

11
00:01:54,315 --> 00:02:09,806
First, list the reads and writes. The add reads x1 and x3, and writes x1. The load word reads x2 and writes x1. The store word reads x1 and x3. The subtract reads x2 and x1, and writes x4.

12
00:02:10,256 --> 00:02:24,787
RAW pairs. Instructions 3 and 4 read x1. Their most recent older writer is instruction 2. So the RAW pairs are 2 3 and 2 4. The pair 1 3 is not RAW, because instruction 2 writes x1 in between.

13
00:02:25,237 --> 00:02:32,027
WAW pairs. Instructions 1 and 2 both write x1. So 1 2 is a WAW pair.

14
00:02:32,477 --> 00:02:40,700
WAR pairs. Instruction 1 reads x1, and then instruction 2 writes x1. So 1 2 is also a WAR pair.

15
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:53,705
The answer: RAW 2 3 and 2 4, WAW 1 2, and WAR 1 2.

16
00:02:54,155 --> 00:03:02,497
Slide 16 asks a question. The or instruction does not use the data memory. Could it skip M and reach W in cycle 5?

17
00:03:02,947 --> 00:03:13,739
The add before it is also in W in cycle 5. Both need the register-file write port. This is a structural hazard: two instructions use one hardware resource at the same time.

18
00:03:14,189 --> 00:03:24,443
There are two fixes. The instructions take turns, so some must stall. Or you add more hardware. ISAs are designed to avoid structural hazards.

19
00:03:24,893 --> 00:03:31,962
Our pipeline uses a simple rule: every instruction goes through all 5 stages. One instruction still finishes per cycle.

20
00:03:32,412 --> 00:03:43,508
Slide 18 looks at the register file. D reads up to two registers, and W writes one. Two read ports and one write port allow all three accesses in one cycle.

21
00:03:43,958 --> 00:03:50,185
Slide 19 looks at memory. Assume one memory for instructions and data. Here are four instructions.

22
00:03:50,635 --> 00:03:58,678
In cycle 4, the load word is in M and reads data from memory. The store word is in F and reads its instruction from the same memory.

23
00:03:59,128 --> 00:04:09,514
Slide 20 gives three solutions: separate memories or caches, a memory with 2 accesses per cycle, or a stall. Our pipeline has IMEM and DMEM.

24
00:04:09,964 --> 00:04:22,410
Real programs pass values through registers. Slide 24 asks: does this sequence execute correctly? All three later instructions read x3, and the add writes x3.

25
00:04:22,860 --> 00:04:27,795
The add writes x3 into the register file in its W stage, in cycle 5.

26
00:04:28,245 --> 00:04:35,826
The load word reads x3 in D, in cycle 3. That is two cycles too early. It gets the wrong value.

27
00:04:36,276 --> 00:04:42,546
The add immediate reads x3 in cycle 4. That is one cycle too early. It also gets the wrong value.

28
00:04:42,996 --> 00:04:53,364
The store word reads x3 in cycle 5, the same cycle as the write. The slide says: maybe, but not in our case. D sees only writes of earlier cycles.

29
00:04:53,814 --> 00:05:01,664
Slide 26 asks about memory data hazards. A load word follows a store word to the same address. Does the load get the right value?

30
00:05:02,114 --> 00:05:13,628
Yes. The store writes the data memory in M, in cycle 4. The load reads it in M, in cycle 5. Both accesses are in the same stage, so they stay in program order.

31
00:05:14,078 --> 00:05:24,586
Registers are different. The register write happens in W, three stages after the register read in D. That is why data hazards occur through registers, and not through memory.

32
00:05:25,036 --> 00:05:43,874
Slide 23 gives three approaches. The first is interlock: the hardware blocks the dependent instruction until the hazard clears. Without forwarding, the subtract reads x1 in D only after the add's W in cycle 5. So it repeats D three times, and reads x1 in cycle 6.

33
00:05:44,324 --> 00:05:54,508
The second is bypass, or forwarding. The add has its result at the end of X, in cycle 3. The hardware sends it straight to the subtract in X, in cycle 4.

34
00:05:54,958 --> 00:06:03,327
The third is speculation: guess the value, and correct the work if the guess was wrong. This video uses interlock and bypass.

35
00:06:03,777 --> 00:06:14,790
Slide 28 makes an observation. The add computes x3 at the end of X, in cycle 3. But it writes x3 into the register file only in W, in cycle 5.

36
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:25,580
Follow the value. In cycle 4, x3 is in the X slash M register. In cycle 5, it is in M slash W. From cycle 6, it is in the register file.

37
00:06:26,030 --> 00:06:39,017
The load word read the wrong x3 in D. But it uses x3 only at the start of X, in cycle 4. Then x3 is in X slash M. Taking it from there is an MX forward.

38
00:06:39,467 --> 00:06:51,463
The add immediate uses x3 in X in cycle 5. Then x3 is in M slash W: a WX forward. Forwarding reads a value from a pipeline register instead of the register file.

39
00:06:51,913 --> 00:07:03,950
Here is the pipelined datapath from video 8. The new parts are the two muxes in front of the ALU, ringed in orange. Each mux selects the value from D slash X, or a value from a later pipeline register.

40
00:07:04,400 --> 00:07:11,830
The MX path takes the ALU result from X slash M back to the first ALU input, mux A, as on slide 29.

41
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:21,016
The WX path takes the value from M slash W back to mux A, as on slide 31. It can be an ALU result or loaded data.

42
00:07:21,466 --> 00:07:33,917
Slides 32 to 34 look at the second ALU input, mux B. An R-type instruction sends rs2 to the ALU. So mux B also gets the MX and WX paths.

43
00:07:34,367 --> 00:07:46,986
Slides 35 to 38 add the WM path, from M slash W to the store-data input of the data memory. A store needs its data only at the start of M.

44
00:07:47,436 --> 00:07:57,108
WM does not go to the address input. The ALU computes the address in X. In the course datapath, store data has only the WM path.

45
00:07:57,558 --> 00:08:07,476
Here is the MX forward of slide 40. In cycle 3, the add is in X and computes x1. The subtract is in D and reads the old x1.

46
00:08:07,926 --> 00:08:20,048
In cycle 4, the add is in M, so x1 is in X slash M. The subtract is in X, and its mux takes x1 from X slash M. The green arrow goes from M to X.

47
00:08:20,498 --> 00:08:28,551
So with MX forwarding, the subtract does not stall. In a diagram, the from stage and the to stage are in the same cycle.

48
00:08:29,001 --> 00:08:37,661
Now the WX forward of slide 40. A load word is between the add and the subtract. In cycle 4, the subtract is in D.

49
00:08:38,111 --> 00:08:50,781
In cycle 5, the add is in W, so x1 is in M slash W. The subtract is in X, and its mux takes x1 from M slash W. The blue arrow goes from W to X.

50
00:08:51,231 --> 00:08:56,854
So a consumer two instructions after an ALU producer gets the value by WX, with no stall.

51
00:08:57,304 --> 00:09:10,974
Now the WM forward of slide 40. The store word stores x1, the result of the add. Here x1 is the store data, not the address. In cycle 4, the store is in X and does not need x1 yet.

52
00:09:11,424 --> 00:09:23,619
In cycle 5, the add is in W, so x1 is in M slash W. The store is in M and needs the data now. WM sends x1 into the data input of the memory.

53
00:09:24,069 --> 00:09:31,166
So the store reads its data at the start of M. The address register, x5, needs no forward.

54
00:09:31,616 --> 00:09:47,591
To understand stores, look at what a store does with its two registers. Store word x 3, 0 of x 4, writes x 3 into memory at address x 4 plus 0. The register rs1, here x 4, gives the address.

55
00:09:48,041 --> 00:09:58,583
The address goes into the ALU in X, so the store needs rs1 at the start of X. The ALU input has a mux, so it can take x 4 by MX or by WX.

56
00:09:59,033 --> 00:10:18,883
The register rs2, here x 3, is the store data. It goes into the data input of the memory in M. So the store needs rs2 later, at the start of M. In X, rs2 passes from D slash X to X slash M with no mux. Its only forwarding path is WM.

57
00:10:19,333 --> 00:10:26,717
Now the producer side. An ALU instruction has its result at the end of X. A load has its value only at the end of M.

58
00:10:27,167 --> 00:10:38,080
So a forward works only if two things are true in the cycle of the need. The value is in a pipeline register. And a path goes from that register to the input that needs it. Now three cases.

59
00:10:38,530 --> 00:10:53,716
Case 1. The load writes x 3, and the store writes x 3 into memory. So x 3 is the store data. In cycle 3, the store is in D and reads the old x 3. That is not a problem, because the store does not use x 3 before M.

60
00:10:54,166 --> 00:11:08,470
In cycle 4, the load is in M. It reads the memory, so x 3 is ready at the end of cycle 4. The green ring marks this. The store is in X and computes its address from x 4. It does not need x 3 yet.

61
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:26,753
In cycle 5, the load is in W, so x 3 is in M slash W. The store is in M and needs x 3 now, as the orange ring shows. The WM path sends x 3 from M slash W to the data input. Ready in cycle 4, needed in cycle 5. So there is no stall.

62
00:11:27,203 --> 00:11:39,974
Case 2. Now the store uses x 3 as its address. Without a stall, the store would be in X in cycle 4. The address goes into the ALU, so it needs x 3 at the start of cycle 4.

63
00:11:40,424 --> 00:11:50,176
But in cycle 4, the load is in M. The value x 3 is ready only at the end of cycle 4. The need comes before the value exists. No forwarding path can fix that.

64
00:11:50,626 --> 00:11:56,245
So the store stalls for one cycle. In cycle 3, it repeats D. This is a D star.

65
00:11:56,695 --> 00:12:11,881
In cycle 5, the load is in W, so x 3 is in M slash W. The store is in X, and the ALU mux takes x 3 by WX. One stall. This is the load-use case, which comes later in this video.

66
00:12:12,331 --> 00:12:26,727
Case 3. The add writes x 1, and the store word two instructions later stores x 1. So x 1 is store data. On the left is the diagram without stalls. Look for a cycle where a path can bring x 1 to the store.

67
00:12:27,177 --> 00:12:38,277
Cycle 4. The store is in D, and the add is in M, so x 1 is in X slash M. But no path goes into D. The store reads the old x 1 from the register file.

68
00:12:38,727 --> 00:12:52,148
Cycle 5. The store is in X, and the add is in W, so x 1 is in M slash W. The WX path goes only to the ALU inputs. The store data passes X with no mux. So WX cannot help.

69
00:12:52,598 --> 00:13:06,762
Cycle 6. The store is in M, where WM could help. But WM takes the value from M slash W, and the add has left the pipeline. x 1 is now only in the register file. So WM is too late.

70
00:13:07,212 --> 00:13:25,695
So no path reaches the store data. The store must read x 1 from the register file in D, in a cycle after the add writes it in cycle 5. That is D in cycle 6, after two D stars. The value was ready early, at the end of cycle 3, but no path could deliver it.

71
00:13:26,145 --> 00:13:38,730
Here are the three cases together. Case 1: the loaded value is store data. It is ready at the end of M in cycle 4 and needed at the start of M in cycle 5. WM forwards it. No stall.

72
00:13:39,180 --> 00:13:52,369
Case 2: the loaded value is the address. It is needed at the start of X in cycle 4, the same cycle it becomes ready at the end. Too late, so one stall, then WX.

73
00:13:52,819 --> 00:14:04,197
Case 3: store data from two instructions ahead. It is ready early, but no path reaches the store data. So the store reads it in D after the producer writes it. Two stalls.

74
00:14:04,647 --> 00:14:17,975
This is the course rule from slides 38 and 39. Store data uses only WM, because rs2 passes X with no mux. If WM is too late, the store reads the value in D, after the producer writes it.

75
00:14:18,425 --> 00:14:34,447
Fall 2019 question 2 part B asks you to add a forwarding path that removes these stalls. A WX path into the store data does it: in case 3, the store gets x 1 in cycle 5 with no stall. Video 10 solves this question.

76
00:14:34,897 --> 00:14:44,559
Slide 39 adds forwarding control logic that sets the muxes. Input 0 is the ALU result in X slash M. Input 1 is the value in M slash W.

77
00:14:45,009 --> 00:15:11,597
Here is the logic for mux A. If rs1 in D slash X equals rd in X slash M, select input 0, the MX path. If rs1 equals rd in M slash W, select input 1, the WX path. In the example of slide 40, the subtract in X has rs1 x1. The add in X slash M has rd x1. They match, so ASel is 0.

78
00:15:12,047 --> 00:15:19,253
Each mux has its own logic. The mux of the second ALU input compares rs2 in the same way.

79
00:15:19,703 --> 00:15:42,393
A check question. The add and the add immediate both write x1, and the subtract reads x1. In cycle 5, X slash M holds the add immediate, and M slash W holds the add. Both rd fields match rs1. Pick X slash M. The add immediate is younger, so it is the most recent writer.

80
00:15:42,843 --> 00:15:50,513
One more case. Here the consumer is three instructions after the load. The load writes x1 in W, in cycle 5.

81
00:15:50,963 --> 00:16:02,835
The subtract is in D in the same cycle 5. D sees only values written in earlier cycles, and there is no forwarding into D. So the subtract reads the old x1.

82
00:16:03,285 --> 00:16:13,922
So the subtract repeats D once: a D star in cycle 5. In cycle 6, it reads the new x1. The lab note on slide 60 calls this the WD hazard.

83
00:16:14,372 --> 00:16:24,665
Fall 2019 question 2 states this rule: no forwarding into the decode stage. Some old quizzes assume otherwise, so state your assumption in the exam.

84
00:16:25,115 --> 00:16:39,079
This table collects the rules, by the distance d from the producer to the consumer. For an ALU result used by the ALU: at distance 1, MX. At 2, WX. At 3, the WD case: one stall.

85
00:16:39,529 --> 00:16:53,434
For a load result used by the ALU: at distance 1, one stall, then WX. This is the load-use case. At 2, WX. At 3, the WD stall.

86
00:16:53,884 --> 00:17:05,269
For store data: at distance 1, WM. At 2, two stalls, because store data has only WM. At 3, the WD stall.

87
00:17:05,719 --> 00:17:18,647
To read a diagram, find the cycle where the consumer needs the value: its X, or its M for store data. Look at the producer in that column. M means MX. W means WX or WM.

88
00:17:19,097 --> 00:17:30,768
Now practice the exam format with the sequence of slide 24. Full forwarding, no forwarding into D. Draw the diagram, and fill both tables. Pause the video and try it.

89
00:17:31,218 --> 00:17:41,747
Cycle 4. The load word is in X and needs x3 for its address. The add is in M. So x3 goes by MX.

90
00:17:42,197 --> 00:17:54,929
Cycle 5. The add immediate is in X, and the add is in W: WX. The store word is in D in the add's W cycle. That is the WD case, so the store repeats D.

91
00:17:55,379 --> 00:18:01,850
The store reads x3 in D in cycle 6, from the register file. The program ends in cycle 9.

92
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:12,955
The forwarding table has one row per forward. Row 1: source 1, destination 2, x3, from M to X, in cycle 4.

93
00:18:13,405 --> 00:18:19,948
Row 2: source 1, destination 3, x3, from W to X, in cycle 5.

94
00:18:20,398 --> 00:18:27,441
The stall table has one row: instruction 4 stalls in D in cycle 5. The reason is the WD case.

95
00:18:27,891 --> 00:18:38,895
One more practice: a load, a store of the loaded value, an add, and a subtract. Use the same rules. Pause the video and try it.

96
00:18:39,345 --> 00:18:55,935
Cycle 5. The load is in W. The store is in M and needs x5 as store data: WM. The add is in X: WX. The subtract is in D in the load's W cycle, so it stalls: the WD case.

97
00:18:56,385 --> 00:19:05,702
The subtract also needs x6 from the add. Without the stall, that is MX. But in cycle 7, the add is in W. So x6 goes by WX.

98
00:19:06,152 --> 00:19:08,259
The program ends in cycle 9.

99
00:19:08,709 --> 00:19:16,012
The forwarding table has three rows. Row 1: x5 goes from the load to the store by WM, in cycle 5.

100
00:19:16,462 --> 00:19:22,253
Row 2: x5 also goes from the load to the add by WX, in cycle 5.

101
00:19:22,703 --> 00:19:28,139
Row 3: x6 goes from the add to the subtract by WX, in cycle 7.

102
00:19:28,589 --> 00:19:39,728
The stall table has one row: instruction 4 stalls in D in cycle 5, the WD case. Lesson: a stall can change the forwarding path. Draw the stalls first.

103
00:19:40,178 --> 00:19:54,553
Some data hazards remain. Slide 43 shows a load followed by a dependent add. The load has its value at the end of M, in cycle 4. The add needs it at the start of X, also in cycle 4. That is too late.

104
00:19:55,003 --> 00:20:04,278
So the hardware detects this load-use case and injects one stall. In cycle 3, the add repeats D, and the subtract repeats F.

105
00:20:04,728 --> 00:20:10,158
In cycle 4, the add is in D again. A bubble, which does nothing, goes into X.

106
00:20:10,608 --> 00:20:17,031
In cycle 5, the load is in W and the add is in X. Now WX forwards the loaded value.

107
00:20:17,481 --> 00:20:24,121
Slide 47 shows the result. The load-use case costs one stall cycle, then a WX forward.

108
00:20:24,571 --> 00:20:37,412
Now the stall hardware, with the load-use example of slide 47. In cycle 3, the load is in X. The add is in D and needs x3, the load's result. The subtract is in F.

109
00:20:37,862 --> 00:20:45,235
At the end of cycle 3, the hardware writes a nop into D slash X. So in cycle 4, a bubble goes into X.

110
00:20:45,685 --> 00:20:58,024
It also disables the write enables of F slash D and the PC. So F slash D keeps the add, and the PC keeps the subtract. In cycle 5, the add gets x3 by WX.

111
00:20:58,474 --> 00:21:27,730
Slide 49 gives the stall condition. The instruction in D slash X is a load, and its rd equals rs1 of the instruction in F slash D. Or its rd equals rs2, and that instruction is not a store. A store does not stall for rs2: WM forwards store data in time. Here, in cycle 3, D slash X holds the load, with rd x3. The add has rs2 x3, and it is not a store. So Stall is 1.

112
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:39,090
Slide 52 calculates the cost of load-use stalls. 20 percent of the instructions are loads. Half of the loads are followed by a dependent instruction. No branch is taken. Pause the video and try it.

113
00:21:39,540 --> 00:21:45,923
Look at 10 instructions. Two are loads. One of them is followed by a dependent instruction. That costs one bubble.

114
00:21:46,373 --> 00:21:50,793
So 10 instructions take 11 cycles. The CPI is 1.1.

115
00:21:51,243 --> 00:22:12,010
With the formula: the CPI is 1, plus 1 stall cycle times 20 percent loads times 50 percent dependent. The 1 stall cycle is the bubble. 20 percent is 2 loads of 10. 50 percent is 1 of the 2 loads. That is 1 plus point 1, or 1.1.

116
00:22:12,460 --> 00:22:22,386
Slide 53 adds a 4-cycle multiply. Its stages P0 to P3 take the place of X and M. A state machine controls the pipeline.

117
00:22:22,836 --> 00:22:35,645
The add immediate needs x4 from the multiply. The product exists at the end of P3, in cycle 6. So the add immediate waits three cycles in D. In cycle 7, WX forwards x4.

118
00:22:36,095 --> 00:22:44,169
The multiplier is often pipelined. Its registers and ALUs are replicated, so a new multiply can start in consecutive cycles.

119
00:22:44,619 --> 00:22:55,167
Slide 55 shows what can go wrong. The add immediate and the add do not depend on the multiply. But the add reaches W in cycle 7, together with the multiply.

120
00:22:55,617 --> 00:23:04,497
Both write the register file in cycle 7: a structural hazard on the single write port. Also, younger instructions finish before the older multiply.

121
00:23:04,947 --> 00:23:16,099
Slides 56 and 57 show a worse case. The multiply and the add immediate both write x4. The add immediate reaches W in cycle 6, before the older multiply in cycle 7.

122
00:23:16,549 --> 00:23:27,088
So the writes to x4 are in the wrong order: a WAW hazard. The add should get x4 from the add immediate, but it gets the product. Deeper pipelines make this common.

123
00:23:27,538 --> 00:23:34,491
Slide 58 shows the corrected diagram. Its stall logic prevents mis-ordered writes to the same register.

124
00:23:34,941 --> 00:23:45,737
The add immediate now waits two cycles in D. So its W is in cycle 8, after the multiply's W in cycle 7. The writes to x4 are in program order.

125
00:23:46,187 --> 00:23:55,360
Now look at the add. It is in D in the W cycle of the add immediate. This is the WD case, so the add repeats D and reads x4 in cycle 9.

126
00:23:55,810 --> 00:24:07,910
Slide 60 is a note for the lab. Add bypass paths to the branch compare unit. When several paths are enabled, pick the correct one. Consider the WD structural hazard. The lab work itself is yours.

127
00:24:08,360 --> 00:24:21,877
Here is the recap. One. For a pair a b, with a older: RAW means b reads what a wrote. WAR means b writes what a reads. WAW means both write.

128
00:24:22,327 --> 00:24:27,475
Two. A structural hazard means that two instructions need one resource in one cycle.

129
00:24:27,925 --> 00:24:34,434
Three. An ALU value exists at the end of X. A load value exists at the end of M.

130
00:24:34,884 --> 00:24:40,502
Four. MX and WX feed the ALU. WM feeds only store data.

131
00:24:40,952 --> 00:24:47,136
Five. There is no forwarding into D. A consumer in D in its producer's W cycle repeats D.

132
00:24:47,586 --> 00:24:57,690
Six. A load-use costs one stall, then WX. The CPI is 1 plus the stall cycles times their fractions.

133
00:24:58,140 --> 00:25:04,030
Seven. Store data from two instructions ahead costs two stalls. Draw the stalls first.

134
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:11,367
Eight. With multi-cycle units, stall so that register writes stay in order. That is the end of video 9.
